{{also|Refrain}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɹɪˈfɹeɪn/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-refrain.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|eɪn|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
{{PIE root|en|bʰreg|dʰer}}
From a combination of {{bor|en|xno|refraindre}}, {{bor|en|frm|refreindre}} (from {{der|en|la|refringere}}), and {{bor|en|xno|refrener}}, {{bor|en|frm|refrener}} (from {{der|en|la|refrenare}}).

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[hold]] back, to [[restrain]] (someone or something). {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{rfdatek|Bible}} Proverbs i:15
#*: '''Refrain''' thy foot from their path.
# {{lb|en|reflexive|archaic}} To show [[restraint]]; to hold oneself back. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* '''1526''', {{w|William Tyndale}}, trans. ''Bible'', Acts V:
#*: And nowe I saye unto you: '''refrayne''' youreselves from these men, and let them alone [...].
# {{lb|en|transitive|now|_|rare}} To [[repress]] (a desire, emotion etc.); to [[check]] or [[curb]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* [[w:Geoffrey_Chaucer|Chaucer]]
#*: his reson aperceyueth it wel that it is synne / agayns the lawe of god / and yet his reson / '''refreyneth''' nat his foul delit or talent.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To stop oneself {{m|en|from}} some [[action]] or [[interference]]; to [[abstain]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{rfdatek|Bible}}, Acts v. 38
#*: '''Refrain''' from these men, and let them alone.
#* {{rfdatek|Thomas Browne}}
#*: They '''refrained''' therefrom [eating flesh] some time after.
# {{lb|en|transitive|now|_|rare|regional}} To [[abstain]] from (food or drink). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{rfdatek|Thomas Browne}}
#*: Who, requiring a remedy for his gout, received no other counsel than to '''refrain''' cold drink.

